WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.930
Morning, Ziad.

00:00:00.930 --> 00:00:02.524
Tuesday the seventeenth.

00:00:02.524 --> 00:00:07.638
A clear, quiet one, with a plain workday on the calendar and an easy evening.

00:00:07.638 --> 00:00:10.095
Here's one to chew on while you move.

00:00:10.095 --> 00:00:15.143
Why does a camera sensor use two green filters for every red and every blue?

00:00:15.143 --> 00:00:16.471
Answer's at the end.

00:00:16.471 --> 00:00:18.663
Weather's nothing to plan around.

00:00:18.663 --> 00:00:24.973
Sunny and dry, a cold start near forty, up to the upper fifties, with only a light west breeze.

00:00:24.973 --> 00:00:28.161
It stays sunny and keeps warming through Friday.

00:00:28.161 --> 00:00:29.091
Okay, the day.

00:00:29.091 --> 00:00:33.009
Calendar has an easy zone two run at seven, thirty minutes.

00:00:33.009 --> 00:00:34.869
Then Godinger, nine to five.

00:00:34.869 --> 00:00:41.511
There's a call at five on your keep-an-eye-on calendar, the one for things that aren't really yours.

00:00:41.511 --> 00:00:43.503
Past that, the evening's open.

00:00:43.503 --> 00:00:44.433
Alright, news.

00:00:44.433 --> 00:00:45.828
One from your corner.

00:00:45.828 --> 00:00:50.079
Relight is now out of beta and in the full version of Photoshop.

00:00:50.079 --> 00:00:59.178
It lets you add up to three virtual lights to a photo after you've taken it, and they sit on their own layer, so you can switch them off.

00:00:59.178 --> 00:01:01.303
The Remove tool got smarter too.

00:01:01.303 --> 00:01:06.284
Take out an object, and it now takes the shadow and the reflection with it.

00:01:06.284 --> 00:01:10.402
On shiny product shots, that second part could save real time.

00:01:10.402 --> 00:01:11.598
A big one for A.I.

00:01:11.598 --> 00:01:11.996
tools.

00:01:11.996 --> 00:01:16.513
OpenAI launched something called ChatGPT Wallet, which lets its A.I.

00:01:16.513 --> 00:01:18.771
agent actually buy things for you.

00:01:18.771 --> 00:01:24.018
You add a card once, then approve every purchase with your face or fingerprint.

00:01:24.018 --> 00:01:27.339
The agent and the store never see the card number.

00:01:27.339 --> 00:01:35.375
It's built with Visa, Mastercard, and American Express, and for now it's only for people on the top paid plan in the U.S.

00:01:35.375 --> 00:01:39.891
Expect a lot of arguing about who pays when it buys the wrong thing.

00:01:39.891 --> 00:01:44.341
And an update on Friday's grounded flights, since we now know more.

00:01:44.341 --> 00:01:51.448
The full stop lasted about four hours, and more than twelve thousand flights got canceled over the weekend.

00:01:51.448 --> 00:01:56.894
The Transportation Secretary is blaming a maintenance contractor, not the new A.I.

00:01:56.894 --> 00:01:59.551
routing system people were asking about.

00:01:59.551 --> 00:02:04.665
He wants another fifty billion dollars from Congress for air traffic control.

00:02:04.665 --> 00:02:12.436
Democrats say about twelve and a half billion was already approved, and only around one billion of it has been spent.

00:02:12.436 --> 00:02:14.628
Okay, here's one for next Monday.

00:02:14.628 --> 00:02:22.930
There's a figure drawing night in a studio building on Jefferson Street in Bushwick, Monday the twenty-third at seven-thirty.

00:02:22.930 --> 00:02:26.583
It's ten bucks, and it looks like it runs most Mondays.

00:02:26.583 --> 00:02:31.166
Drawing a live model for an evening is a sneaky good lighting lesson.

00:02:31.166 --> 00:02:39.866
You spend two hours watching exactly how light wraps around a body, and you don't need to be good at drawing to get that out of it.

00:02:39.866 --> 00:02:42.124
Today's challenge is a social one.

00:02:42.124 --> 00:02:48.102
Think of someone you haven't talked to in a few months, and send them one specific memory.

00:02:48.102 --> 00:02:49.630
Not "hey, how are you".

00:02:49.630 --> 00:02:54.146
Something like, remember that time we got lost looking for that bar.

00:02:54.146 --> 00:02:59.526
Specific memories get real replies, and it takes thirty seconds once you're home.

00:02:59.526 --> 00:03:01.983
Okay, something you've been learning.

00:03:01.983 --> 00:03:05.835
Remember setting the memo's audio to that target loudness?

00:03:05.835 --> 00:03:11.149
You already know how loudness gets measured, and why there's a ceiling on peaks.

00:03:11.149 --> 00:03:12.676
Today's the other half.

00:03:12.676 --> 00:03:21.643
Why the loud and quiet parts inside one episode matter more than the overall number, especially for you, right now, with one earbud in.

00:03:21.643 --> 00:03:25.694
There's a measurement for that spread, called loudness range.

00:03:25.694 --> 00:03:33.996
It's the gap between the quieter stretches and the louder stretches of a recording, in the same units as the loudness target.

00:03:33.996 --> 00:03:36.985
A film can swing from whispers to explosions.

00:03:36.985 --> 00:03:43.162
On a street, the whispers vanish under a bus, so you turn it up, and then the explosion hits.

00:03:43.162 --> 00:03:46.549
For listening on the move, you want a narrow range.

00:03:46.549 --> 00:03:52.062
Everything sits in a tight band, so one volume setting works the whole way through.

00:03:52.062 --> 00:03:54.718
The tool that does that is a compressor.

00:03:54.718 --> 00:03:59.833
You set a threshold, and anything louder than it gets turned down by a ratio.

00:03:59.833 --> 00:04:04.814
Two to one means for every two steps over the line, only one comes through.

00:04:04.814 --> 00:04:10.791
Then you raise the whole thing back up, so the quiet parts end up closer to the loud ones.

00:04:10.791 --> 00:04:13.050
Here's the nice part for the memo.

00:04:13.050 --> 00:04:13.581
A T.T.S.

00:04:13.581 --> 00:04:15.374
voice is already very even.

00:04:15.374 --> 00:04:18.695
No one leans away from the mic or laughs too loud.

00:04:18.695 --> 00:04:24.274
So it barely needs compressing, which is part of why it's easy to hear over traffic.

00:04:24.274 --> 00:04:29.720
Normalization just sets the overall level, and the voice was steady to begin with.

00:04:29.720 --> 00:04:34.569
So: calendar says run at seven and Godinger till five, then a free night.

00:04:34.569 --> 00:04:36.030
Send someone a memory.

00:04:36.030 --> 00:04:42.273
The answer: your eyes pull most of their detail from green, so the sensor spends double on it.

00:04:42.273 --> 00:04:44.000
Have a good Tuesday, Ziad.
